Just goes to show that one should be careful taking what Wikipaedia
claims as the complete truth[1], and that myths vary according to who
tells them. Homeros considers Asklepios to be a human; Hesiodos takes
him to be a god or half-god, son of Apollon and Koronis. In the earliest
myths, Asklepios seems to _be_ the serpent himself, and later on it's
still one of his incarnations.
(Also note that even where he was human, his consecration in the skies
would just about have made him a demi-god anyway.)

